#
# clang-tidy configuration for ntlibc.  Consumed by tools/lint.sh (`make
# lint`), which is opt-in and never part of the normal tcc build.
#
# Enable all checks in the four families this project has selected.  Findings
# that are inherent to implementing a freestanding C library or an external
# ABI are suppressed at the narrowest declaration/region with their rationale;
# keeping those exceptions beside the code lets every newly added family check
# run by default instead of growing a central blind spot here.
#
# The disable list below is the SECOND time most of these entries have
# existed.  c1edbb02/685809f2 first wrote them, with per-check rationale,
# when `make lint` was added; 533b9541/82bf2cfc ("lint: enable the full
# clang-tidy families") deleted the whole list on the theory that every
# finding could instead be suppressed narrowly, in place, with NOLINT.  That
# theory holds for a false positive that shows up once or twice -- it is
# exactly how e.g. bugprone-reserved-identifier/cert-dcl37-c/cert-dcl51-cpp
# stay enabled and handled per-header below, and how 3d553524 fixed three
# genuine one-off analyzer false positives.  It does not hold for a check
# whose *entire premise* is wrong for this codebase: every one of the checks
# below fired on ~10 to ~1500 distinct call sites apiece (2026-09-06 full-tree
# triage, clang-tidy-18, all three arches; a re-run tools/lint.sh analyze
# reproduces the exact counts cited per check), which is not "a false
# positive here and there", it is the check itself being the wrong tool for
# this tree.  Each entry below is restored with concrete, re-verified
# evidence from that triage, not merely by reference to the original commit.
#
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  misc-*,

  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-header-include-cycle,

  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-narrowing-conversions,
  -bugprone-assignment-in-if-condition,
  -bugprone-branch-clone,
  -bugprone-macro-parentheses,
  -bugprone-switch-missing-default-case,
  -bugprone-suspicious-string-compare,
  -bugprone-casting-through-void,
  -bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-conversion,
  -bugprone-unsafe-functions,
  -bugprone-signed-char-misuse,

  -cert-str34-c,
  -cert-msc24-c,
  -cert-msc33-c,
  -cert-err33-c,
  -cert-env33-c,

  -clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.strcpy,

  -misc-no-recursion,

# Rationale, check by check.  Finding counts are from the 2026-09-06 triage
# (three arches, clang-tidy-18, LINT_TIDY_EXACT=1) and will drift as the tree
# grows; they are cited to show scale, not as a promise to keep them exact.
#
#   misc-include-cleaner, misc-header-include-cycle (1487 findings, all three
#       arches, misc-include-cleaner alone -- header-include-cycle currently
#       fires zero times but shares the identical root cause, see below)
#       Both need a compilation database and a hosted "one header, one
#       provider" include model; neither applies to a -nostdinc freestanding
#       build with generated, per-arch, internal-only headers.  Two
#       independent, reproducible malfunction classes were found, not just
#       borderline judgment calls:
#         1. Every file in src/complex/ that defines a `double complex`/
#            `float complex` function (cexp.c, ctrig.c, cinverse.c,
#            complex_parts.c, complex_rtlib.c -- 100% of the directory, 759
#            of the 1487 findings) gets EVERY identifier in the file flagged,
#            including a function's own name at its own definition (e.g.
#            "no header providing \"clog\" is directly included" pointing at
#            `double complex clog(double complex z)`'s own definition line)
#            and its own local variables (e.g. "no header providing \"wr\" is
#            directly included" pointing at `double wr = yr * lr - yi * li;`,
#            a plain local declared and used entirely inside that one
#            function).  No header can ever "provide" a symbol's own
#            definition or a local variable; this is not a suggestion that
#            can be acted on.
#         2. Every fundamental type this tree defines through its musl-
#            derived `#define __NEED_size_t` + `bits/alltypes.h` umbrella
#            mechanism (size_t, time_t, struct tm, regex_t, ...) is reported
#            as "no header providing X is directly included" even in files
#            that already directly include the correct, canonical, public
#            umbrella header for it (verified on src/string/memchr.c,
#            src/ctype/wctype.c, src/misc/getopt.c, src/sh/func.c and
#            src/netdb/linux/hostent.c: each already `#include`s <string.h>/
#            <wctype.h>/<time.h> directly and still gets flagged), because
#            the checker attributes the typedef to bits/alltypes.h itself --
#            a per-arch file generated into obj/lint/$arch/include/bits/ at
#            lint time, never meant to be named by consumer code at all.
#            misc-include-cleaner's only relevant option, IgnoreHeaders, was
#            tried against `bits/alltypes\.h` and does not suppress this (the
#            base diagnostic fires with or without a suggested header); an
#            IWYU pragma: export on the re-exporting headers was also tried
#            (include/string_tokens.h's `#include <ownership.h>`) and also
#            did not change clang-tidy-18's output.  Neither of these two
#            classes has a source-level fix: there is no header to add.
#       A third, real pattern also showed up in the same triage --
#       withtok(...)/elements_withtok(...)/consume(...) (ownership.h's own
#       macros, ~400 findings) used in a .c file via a project-internal
#       facade header (src/internal/ownership_stubs.h, or a domain header
#       like string.h/netdb.h) that itself includes ownership.h -- but this
#       is this project's OWN deliberate, load-bearing header architecture
#       doing its job (e.g. string.h's own declarations use withtok(...)
#       too, so string.h including string_tokens.h including ownership.h is
#       not an accident anything could "break" by changing), not a fragile
#       accident of transitive inclusion the way the original per-check
#       false-positive precedent (3d553524) means.  Asking ~100 leaf .c files
#       to each separately #include <ownership.h> on top of the domain
#       header that already re-exports it fights this tree's own layering
#       rather than fixing anything.
#   bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#       memcpy(void*, const void*, size_t) is the shape of the entire
#       public API; this check would flag most of the library.  Verified
#       still true today: cpow(double complex x, double complex y)
#       (src/complex/cexp.c) is exactly this shape.
#   bugprone-narrowing-conversions
#       Overlapping with -Wconversion, which lint.sh already offers behind
#       LINT_CONVERSION=1 where the results can be read in one place.
#   bugprone-assignment-in-if-condition, bugprone-branch-clone,
#   bugprone-macro-parentheses
#       Deliberate musl-derived house style.  Verified still true today:
#       src/util/strip.c:547 (assignment-in-if), src/util/man.c:2947
#       (branch-clone), src/regex/regex.c:1155 (macro-parentheses, in the
#       same DFA macro family as the one already NOLINT'd in
#       include/features.h's __REDIR).
#   bugprone-signed-char-misuse
#       ctype and the string routines index tables with (unsigned char)c on
#       purpose; the check does not model that idiom.  Verified still true
#       today: src/util/ed.c:736.
#   bugprone-casting-through-void, bugprone-multi-level-implicit-pointer-
#   conversion
#       Unavoidable when talking to the NT API's void*/PVOID* interfaces, and
#       the same shape shows up at the two platforms' other big external-ABI
#       boundary too: crt/linux/crt1.c:415 casts `char **envp` through
#       `void *` to hand it to the platform's own entry-point contract, and
#       src/dlfcn/linux/plat_dlfcn.c:1318 casts a function pointer through
#       `void *` to a uintptr_t the way dlsym()'s own POSIX-mandated return
#       type requires.
#   bugprone-suspicious-include
#       arch/ and src/*/ legitimately include .h fragments by relative path.
#   bugprone-switch-missing-default-case
#       Several switches here deliberately fall through to code after the
#       switch for the unhandled cases (e.g. wait.c's NT exception decode);
#       that is not an omission, it does not need a `default:` restating it.
#       Verified still true today: src/util/man.c:1661.
#   bugprone-suspicious-string-compare
#       Fires on ordinary `if (strcmp(a, b))`/`if (!strncmp(...))`, which is
#       idiomatic C, not a mistaken comparison against 1 or -1.  Verified
#       still true today: src/util/at.c:175.
#   bugprone-unsafe-functions, cert-msc24-c, cert-msc33-c
#       These flag *implementing* gets()/rand()/asctime()/rewind() and
#       friends.  ntlibc implements them; it cannot be told not to.
#       Verified still true today: src/netdb/linux/hostent.c:150 (defining
#       rewind()'s own caller-facing replacement guidance fires on ntlibc's
#       own internal use of rewind()).
#   cert-str34-c
#       Co-fires with bugprone-signed-char-misuse on the exact same `(int)
#       (signed char)c`-shaped ctype/string idiom (src/util/ed.c:736); same
#       rationale.
#   cert-err33-c (991 findings, the single largest non-include-cleaner
#   category, 327 distinct call sites)
#       "check the return value of this stdlib call" -- inside the stdlib,
#       where malloc/snprintf are called into buffers whose size was already
#       computed and checked (src/internal/rpath.c:117, src/ftw/ftw.c:413),
#       or the result is deliberately discarded because a fixed, pre-sized
#       destination cannot overflow.  A check meant for application code
#       calling libc cannot be usefully applied to libc calling itself.
#   cert-env33-c
#       Flags *implementing* system().  ntlibc implements it.  Verified
#       still true today: src/util/awk_run.c:1755 (awk's own system()
#       builtin, one of several utilities that legitimately shell out).
#   clang-analyzer-security.insecureAPI.strcpy (56 findings, 20 distinct
#   call sites)
#       Every one of the 20 call sites checked (src/misc/nt/grp.c,
#       src/netdb/linux/getnameinfo.c, src/netdb/nt/plat_netdb.c,
#       src/util/ar.c, atd.c, crond.c, m4.c, mailx.c, pax.c, spool.c) is an
#       explicit `if (strlen(src) [+1] > destsz) return/goto fail;` guard
#       immediately above the strcpy() -- a bounds check the checker cannot
#       correlate with the copy two lines later, not an unbounded copy.
#       This is a different, and more precisely verified, rationale than
#       the original list's "these are the definitions, not calls" (most of
#       these 20 sites are genuine calls, not strcpy's own implementation),
#       arrived at independently in the 2026-09-06 triage.
#   misc-no-recursion (240 findings)
#       Recursion is fine and used (e.g. qsort, glob, dlclose's dependency-
#       graph teardown).  Verified still true today:
#       src/dlfcn/linux/plat_dlfcn.c's teardown_obj().
#
# Everything still enabled -- notably the whole clang-analyzer-core.* and
# unix.Malloc set, bugprone-sizeof-expression, bugprone-implicit-widening-
# of-multiplication-result, cert-err34-c, and bugprone-reserved-identifier/
# cert-dcl37-c/cert-dcl51-cpp (handled per-header/per-line with NOLINT,
# never disabled here) -- is there because it finds real bugs in this kind
# of code: uninitialised reads, leaked allocations on error paths, unchecked
# atoi() truncation, and size arithmetic that overflows before it is
# widened (the canonical example this project's own tools/clang-tidy-
# checks.txt cites for why this whole stage exists).
#
HeaderFilterRegex: '(src|include|arch|crt)/.*\.h$'
WarningsAsErrors: ''
FormatStyle: none
